## 3. Core Technology

### Hybrid Operating Model

Under stable conditions the system prefers **linear relay chains** — ordered multi-hop paths that are simple and predictable. When several nodes on an active chain show clear degradation (typically three or more), the system can switch that chain into **local mesh recovery**: collect candidates, apply a progressive fallback ladder, enforce a quality gate, and replace only the affected path. The rest of the network remains untouched. Once conditions improve, operation can return to the linear style.

### Dynamic Chain Relay

PSHMP Core dynamically builds and rebuilds multi-hop delivery chains according to current network conditions. Traffic can be delivered through a sequence of relay nodes rather than relying exclusively on a direct path. When individual nodes or links degrade, the system constructs alternative delivery chains using currently available candidates.

### K-Factor Evaluation

Nodes are evaluated with a composite stability metric (K-Factor). The reference model used in the experimental implementation is:

```text
K = 0.45 × (1 − loss / 0.35)
  + 0.25 × (1 − jitter / 800)
  + 0.20 × (1 − rtt / 1200)
  + 0.10 × battery
```

Terms are clamped to [0, 1]. The resulting score drives both relay selection and early degradation detection. Status bands (EXCELLENT → CRITICAL) and exact weights are configuration parameters and can be tuned by the integrating party.

Candidate ranking uses an additional weighted score:

```text
Score = 0.50 × KScore + 0.25 × LatencyScore + 0.15 × LossScore + 0.10 × GeoScore
```

### Proactive Self-Healing

The core is designed to detect network degradation and initiate route reconstruction before a complete failure occurs. Recovery is prioritised (CRITICAL / HIGH / MEDIUM / LOW) with adaptive cooldown so that the system does not thrash. Under simulated conditions the implementation has shown recovery behaviour on the order of hundreds of milliseconds. Actual recovery time depends on topology, load, and available candidates.

### Local Mesh Recovery and Fallback Ladder

When multiple nodes within the same relay chain become degraded, the affected chain can automatically transition into local mesh recovery. The process uses DHT and Gossip candidates, failure tracking, quarantine, diversity-aware selection, and a multi-level fallback ladder:

```text
Level 0: MinAvgK ≥ 0.70, diversity required
Level 1: MinAvgK ≥ 0.65, diversity required
Level 2: MinAvgK ≥ 0.60, diversity required
Level 3: MinAvgK ≥ 0.52, diversity required
Level 4: MinAvgK ≥ 0.42, diversity optional
```

A quality gate is applied before the new path replaces the old one. Mesh recovery operates locally on the affected chain rather than requiring the entire network to be rebuilt.

### Diversity-Aware Recovery

During recovery, candidates can be selected with preference for different logical failure domains (subnet, region, provider, availability zone, or deployment-defined groups). The objective is not simply to find another available node, but to construct a route with greater structural diversity — especially important when multiple nodes in the same domain degrade together.

### Distributed Candidate Discovery

DHT-based candidate selection is combined with Gossip-based state propagation (push and anti-entropy). This reduces dependence on a single source of network information and allows the system to continue operating when a central coordinator is unavailable. An optional Raft-based coordinator can be used when stronger consistency is required; it is not mandatory.

### Failure Tracking and Quarantine

The core maintains information about node failures and degraded behaviour. Repeatedly unstable nodes can be temporarily quarantined and excluded from new path construction. After a period of stable behaviour a node may be rehabilitated. This reduces recovery loops caused by the same unreliable participants.

### Transport-Efficient Delivery

Batch acknowledgements with an explicit gap list allow the receiver to report the highest contiguous sequence and the missing ranges. The sender retransmits only what is actually lost. Under the traffic patterns examined in internal tests this approach has reduced control-plane overhead substantially (in favourable cases approaching the order of 75%). Exact efficiency depends on loss rate, batch size, and configuration.

### Overlay Deployment Model

PSHMP Core operates as an overlay layer over existing IP infrastructure. The underlying L3 routing can remain unchanged. Supported transports include UDP, DTLS, TLS, and WebRTC DataChannels, with optional TURN assistance for NAT traversal.

